href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2018\/house\/nc\/north_carolina_2nd_district_holding_vs_coleman-6678.html\">NC-2<\/a><span>\u00a0<\/span>|<a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2018\/house\/nc\/north_carolina_8th_district_hudson_vs_mcneill-6650.html\"><span>\u00a0<\/span>NC-8<\/a><span>\u00a0<\/span>|<span>\u00a0<\/span><a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2018\/house\/nc\/north_carolina_9th_district_harris_vs_mccready-6533.html\">NC-9<\/a><span>\u00a0<\/span>|<span>\u00a0<\/span><a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2018\/house\/nc\/north_carolina_13th_district_budd_vs_manning-6500.html\">NC-13<\/a><strong><br \/>2016<span>:<\/span><\/strong><span>\u00a0<\/span><a href=\"http:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2016\/president\/nc\/north_carolina_trump_vs_clinton-5538.html\">President<\/a><span>\u00a0<\/span>|<span>\u00a0<\/span><a href=\"http:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2016\/governor\/nc\/north_carolina_governor_mccrory_vs_cooper-4096.html\">Governor<\/a><strong><br \/>2014<\/strong>:<span><span>\u00a0<\/span><\/span><a href=\"http:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2014\/house\/nc\/north_carolina_2nd_district_ellmers_vs_aiken-5049.html\">NC-2<\/a><span><span>\u00a0<\/span><\/span>|<span><span>\u00a0<\/span><\/span><a href=\"http:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2014\/house\/nc\/north_carolina_7th_district_rouzer_vs_barfield-5038.html\">NC-7<\/a><strong><br \/><\/strong><\/p>"},"dataAnalysis":{"title":"Race Analysis","content":"<p>North Carolina has had a substantial Republican presence in the northwest mountains since the Civil War. The Democratic Party was historically split between progressives and conservatives, and as the national Democratic Party moved leftward, Republicans had an opportunity to fuse the more conservative Democrats with the Republican rump and form a majority.<\/p>\n<p>Jesse Helms was the first Republican to succeed in bringing those conservatives into the GOP. At the same time, northerners flocking to the Research Triangle area brought an even more pronounced Republican bent to the state. For a while, it looked like North Carolina would become a solidly red state.<\/p>\n<p>But moderate Democratic governors like Jim Hunt and Mike Easley kept some of the conservative Democrats in the fold, while suburbanites in the Triange\u00a0area drifted toward the Democratic Party. In 2002, Elizabeth Dole successfully held Helms\u2019 seat against Democrat Erskine Bowles, only to lose to state Sen. Kay Hagan in 2008. Hagan, in turn, lost to Thom Tillis in 2014.<\/p>\n<p>Now the seat is open because Thom Tillis, never popular with his own base, decided not to seek a third term after crossing the president on his taxation\/spending bill. Republicans nominated Michael Whatley, a rare candidate favored both by the president and establishment Republicans. Whatley has never run for office before but chaired the state Republican Party for years. Democrats got the candidate they wanted in former Gov. Roy Cooper, who has won six statewide races and never lost one, including a 2020 gubernatorial race he won while Trump carried the state.<\/p>\n<p>Cooper has led in most polls this year, usually by mid-single digits and sometimes by more. He has also raised substantially more cash than Whatley. Whatley wants to nationalize the race around immigration and crime, but he is a first-time candidate running against the most successful North Carolina Democrat of his generation in a midterm that is breaking against Whatley\u2019s party. Cooper is in the driver\u2019s seat, and Whatley needs the environment to change somehow before he can catch him.<\/p>"},"showLineChart":1,"showBarChart":1,"showLineChartFirst":0,"sideCharts":{"charts_on_top":0,"left_chart_race_id":"6908","right_chart_race_id":"3497"},"show_final_results":0,"chart_data":{"chart_id":"7310","chart_axis_x":"1","chart_grid_x":"4","chart_axis_y":"1","chart_grid_y":"10","chart_min"